# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Use this guide to expose an existing Kubernetes `Service` using an `HTTPRoute`.

By the end, your application will be reachable at:

```text
https://<your-app>.<your-business-unit>.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
```

## What is Gateway API?

Gateway API is the Kubernetes API for service networking. It lets you define how external traffic reaches services in your cluster using resources such as `Gateway` and `HTTPRoute`.

In the older Cloud Platform model, a single nginx `Ingress` resource described both where traffic entered the cluster and how requests were routed to services. With Gateway API, these responsibilities are split across dedicated resources.

See the Kubernetes documentation: [Gateway API](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/gateway/).

## What is Envoy Gateway?

Envoy Gateway is the Kubernetes-native gateway controller built on top of Envoy Proxy. In Container Platform, Envoy Gateway is part of the ingress path that receives requests and forwards them to your service based on your `HTTPRoute`.

In practice, the platform manages the shared traffic entry point (`ListenerSet`, `Gateway`, and Envoy Gateway), while application teams manage `HTTPRoute` rules for their own hostnames and services.

See the Envoy Gateway documentation: [Envoy Gateway docs](https://gateway.envoyproxy.io/docs/).

You will make use of the shared platform `ListenerSet` called `default-listenerset` in the `envoy-gateway-system` namespace. See the [ListenerSet documentation](https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/guides/user-guides/listener-set/) for more information.

## Before you start

Make sure you already have:

- a namespace for your application
- a running workload (for example, a `Deployment`)
- a `Service` in that namespace that points to your workload
- the service port your application listens on

## Steps

### 1. Choose values for your route

You need these values:

- `<your-app>`: route name and hostname prefix
- `<your-hostname>`: route hostname
- `<your-business-unit>`: your business unit, see below for how to find this value
- `<your-namespace>`: your namespace
- `<your-service-name>`: the `Service` name to send traffic to
- `<your-service-port>`: the service port to use

If needed, run `kubectl get svc -n <your-namespace>` to list services in your namespace.

To find valid hostnames that you can use, describe the default `ListenerSet` in the `envoy-gateway-system` namespace:

```sh
kubectl describe listenerset -n envoy-gateway-system default-listenerset | grep "Hostname"

# Example output:
Hostname: *.<octo-nonlive>.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk
```

So any hostname that matches the pattern `*.<your-business-unit>.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk` is valid.

For example, if your business unit is `octo-nonlive`, you could use `my-app.octo-nonlive.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk`.

### 2. Create an HTTPRoute manifest

Create a file named `httproute.yaml` and substitute the values you chose in the previous step:

```yaml
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: <your-app>
  namespace: <your-namespace>
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: default-listenerset
      namespace: envoy-gateway-system
  hostnames:
    - "<your-hostname>"
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - group: ""
          kind: Service
          name: <your-service-name>
          port: <your-service-port>
```

### 3. Apply the route

```sh
kubectl apply -f httproute.yaml
```

### 4. Check that the route is accepted

```sh
kubectl get httproute <your-app> -n <your-namespace>
```

You can also check the `Accepted` condition:

```sh
kubectl describe httproute <your-app> -n <your-namespace>
```

In the output, look for `Type: Accepted` and `Status: True`.

### 5. Open your application URL

Once the route is accepted and DNS has propagated, open your HTTPRoute hostname in a browser or use `curl` to check that your application is reachable.

You can find the hostname in the `hostnames` section of your `HTTPRoute` manifest or in the output of `kubectl describe httproute <your-app> -n <your-namespace>`.

### 6. Optional: remove the route later

```sh
kubectl delete -f httproute.yaml
```

## Full working example manifest

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: my-app
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-app-deployment
  namespace: my-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app
    spec:
      securityContext:
        seccompProfile:
          type: RuntimeDefault
      containers:
        - name: echo
          image: hashicorp/http-echo:0.2.3
          args:
            - "-text=Hello, Cloud Platform!"
          securityContext:
            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
            readOnlyRootFilesystem: true
            runAsNonRoot: true
            capabilities:
              drop:
                - ALL
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-app
  namespace: my-app
spec:
  selector:
    app: my-app
  ports:
    - name: http
      protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
---
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: my-app-route
  namespace: my-app
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - group: gateway.networking.k8s.io
      kind: ListenerSet
      name: default-listenerset
      namespace: envoy-gateway-system
  hostnames:
    - "my-app.octo-nonlive.container-platform.service.justice.gov.uk"
  rules:
    - matches:
        - path:
            type: PathPrefix
            value: /
      backendRefs:
        - group: ""
          kind: Service
          name: my-app
          port: 80
```
